      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C26"],
      "claim": "Bumetanide has about 89% oral bioavailability in normal subjects; high F contradicts the low prediction, but its single-carboxylate/sulfonamide profile does not capture eprosartan's dicarboxylate permeability limitation.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Levodopa shows high absolute bioavailability in most studies; contradictory to the low prediction, but its absorption is transporter-mediated and not representative of eprosartan.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Phenylbutyric acid has 78% absolute bioavailability in humans; contradictory to the low prediction, but it is a simple monocarboxylic acid lacking the query's two carboxylate groups.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is eprosartan, whose established absolute oral bioavailability is approximately 13%, below the 20% threshold. Retrieved analogues are mostly high-F carboxylic acids, but none reproduces the dual-carboxylate/zwitterionic absorption barrier of eprosartan, so they do not overturn the direct drug-level knowledge.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No retrieved record directly measures eprosartan; all supplied analogues report F >=20%.",
    "The low prediction relies on recognizing the query as eprosartan and its established ~13% absolute bioavailability rather than on a supplied low-F analogue."
  ],
